        {
          "title": "1. Moments in Equilibrium",
          "sequenceOrder": 1,
          "status": "published",
          "xpReward": 55,
          "maxReattempts": 3,
          "passingThreshold": 60,
          "estimatedDuration": 10,
          "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
          "learnBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "ol-4-1",
              "type": "blob_dialog",
              "content": "An object in equilibrium has two conditions, and most students only remember one.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "id": "l1",
                    "text": "An object in equilibrium has two conditions, and most students only remember one.",
                    "mood": "thinking",
                    "cta": "Which two?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l2",
                    "text": "The forces balance, and the turning effects balance about any point you choose.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "Any point?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l3",
                    "text": "Any point. Choosing the pivot removes an unknown force from the equation, which is why examiners set it there.",
                    "mood": "cheer",
                    "cta": "Clever"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-4-2",
              "type": "lever_lab",
              "content": "Balance the beam, then check that clockwise and anticlockwise moments are equal.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "notches": 5,
                "weights": [
                  2,
                  4,
                  6
                ],
                "left": {
                  "position": -4,
                  "weight": 2
                },
                "right": null,
                "caption": "In equilibrium the clockwise and anticlockwise moments about the pivot are equal."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-4-3",
              "type": "formula",
              "content": "\\sum M_{clockwise} = \\sum M_{anticlockwise}"
            }
          ],
          "evaluateBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "ol-eb-4-1",
              "type": "tap_target",
              "question": "A uniform metre rule is pivoted at its centre. A 4 N weight hangs 20 cm from the pivot. What moment must balance it?",
              "correctAnswer": "point-eight",
              "explanation": "Moment = force x distance in metres = 4 x 0.20 = 0.8 N m.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "targets": [
                  {
                    "id": "point-eight",
                    "label": "0.8 N m"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "eighty",
                    "label": "80 N m"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "twenty",
                    "label": "20 N m"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "four",
                    "label": "4 N m"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-eb-4-2",
              "type": "toggle_switch",
              "question": "Switch on the conditions needed for full equilibrium.",
              "correctAnswer": "forces=on,moments=on,symmetry=off",
              "explanation": "Equilibrium needs balanced forces and balanced moments. Symmetry is convenient but never required.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "switches": [
                  {
                    "id": "forces",
                    "label": "The forces balance",
                    "onLabel": "required",
                    "offLabel": "not required"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "moments",
                    "label": "The moments balance",
                    "onLabel": "required",
                    "offLabel": "not required"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "symmetry",
                    "label": "The object is symmetrical",
                    "onLabel": "required",
                    "offLabel": "not required"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-eb-4-3",
              "type": "order_steps",
              "question": "Put the moments question method in order.",
              "correctAnswer": "pivot>list>equate>solve",
              "explanation": "Choosing the pivot first is what removes an unknown from the equation before you write it.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "id": "pivot",
                    "label": "Choose the pivot, ideally through an unknown force"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "list",
                    "label": "List every force and its distance from that pivot"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "equate",
                    "label": "Set clockwise moments equal to anticlockwise"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "solve",
                    "label": "Solve for the unknown"
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "2. Pressure in Solids and Liquids",
          "sequenceOrder": 2,
          "status": "published",
          "xpReward": 55,
          "maxReattempts": 3,
          "passingThreshold": 60,
          "estimatedDuration": 10,
          "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
          "learnBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "ol-5-1",
              "type": "blob_dialog",
              "content": "Pressure is force spread over area, which is why a drawing pin hurts and a plank does not.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "id": "l1",
                    "text": "Pressure is force spread over area, which is why a drawing pin hurts and a plank does not.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "Same force?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l2",
                    "text": "Same force, tiny area. In liquids there is a second formula for depth.",
                    "mood": "thinking",
                    "cta": "Which?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l3",
                    "text": "Pressure equals density times gravity times depth. Note what is missing: the shape of the container.",
                    "mood": "surprised",
                    "cta": "Shape does not matter?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l4",
                    "text": "Not at all. Only depth, density and gravity.",
                    "mood": "cheer",
                    "cta": "Good to know"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-5-2",
              "type": "formula",
              "content": "P = \\frac{F}{A} \\qquad P = \\rho g h"
            },
            {
              "id": "ol-5-3",
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A tank and a thin pipe filled to the same height have the **same pressure at the bottom**. The width of the water above changes the weight, not the pressure."
            }
          ],
          "evaluateBlocks": [
